<p class="text-lg text-silver-400 leading-relaxed mb-6">
  Car insurance is a contract about who pays after a specific kind of loss, up to a limit you chose, minus a deductible you chose. The advertisement is a monthly number. The contract is the declarations page. Two quotes are not comparable until the liability limits, the deductibles, and the VIN are the same, because a cheaper policy that pays $25,000 when you caused $200,000 of harm is not a savings. This hub is how to read that page, how state minimums fit in, and where a quote tool belongs in the process.
</p>

<h2>The coverages, separated</h2>
<p>
  Liability pays other people when you are at fault, up to the limit on the declarations page. It is the coverage every state that requires insurance is talking about, and the required limit is often too low to cover a serious injury. Your wages and your house sit behind a limit that is exhausted. Buying the state minimum meets the law. It does not meet a hospital bill. The National Association of Insurance Commissioners explains the standard coverages for consumers at <a href="https://content.naic.org/consumer/auto-insurance.htm" rel="noopener">content.naic.org</a>. Your state insurance department is the authority for the minimum where you garage the car. Our <a href="/states/">state directory</a> links the minimums we track. Confirm the figure with the department before you rely on it. Legislatures change them.
</p>
<p>
  Collision pays to repair your car after a crash, regardless of fault, subject to the deductible and to the insurer’s right to total the car. Comprehensive pays for the other physical losses the policy names, typically theft, fire, flood, hail, and animal strikes. Both are optional under state law once a loan is paid off, and both are required by almost every lender while a loan or a lease is open. The lender’s requirement is in the loan contract, not in the state statute.
</p>
<p>
  Uninsured and underinsured motorist coverage pays you when the other driver has no insurance or not enough. In some states it is mandatory. In others it is the coverage people decline and then wish they had. Medical payments or personal injury protection, depending on the state, pays medical bills under its own rules and is central in no-fault states. Do not assume the policy you had in the last state still matches the law in this one. A move is a reason to re-read the contract, not only to change the garaging address.
</p>

<h2>Why the same car is two prices</h2>
<p>
  Carriers weight driving record, garaging ZIP code, mileage, credit-based insurance scores where state law allows them, prior coverage, and the car itself. A VIN that decodes to a different engine, a different body, or a performance trim is a different rate. Decode the car with the <a href="/vin-decoder/">VIN decoder</a> before you quote it, so you are not comparing a base-model price against a car that is not the base model. Safety equipment and a strong crash-test result can move a rate. They do not move it by a published national percentage, and anyone who quotes you “save 10 to 30 percent by comparing” is describing a hope. Compare the declarations pages. The dollar difference is the fact.
</p>
<p>
  Our notes on carriers and on ways people actually reduce a premium are in <a href="/guides/insurance/best-car-insurance/">best car insurance companies</a> and <a href="/guides/insurance/cheapest-car-insurance/">cheapest car insurance</a>. Discounts for bundling, for a monitored device, for low mileage, and for paying in full are real at some carriers and absent at others. Ask for each one by name and ask for the revised declarations page. A discount that requires a device sharing your driving is a privacy decision as well as a price.
</p>
<p>
  Deductibles are the lever people understand and then set too low. Raising a comprehensive deductible on a car you could pay to repair is a reasonable way to lower the premium. Raising it on a car you could not pay to repair means the coverage fails at the moment you bought it for. Match the deductible to cash you can produce in a week.
</p>

<h2>Claims, totals, and titles</h2>
<p>
  After a crash, the insurer’s estimate and the shop’s estimate are a negotiation. You can often choose the shop. You cannot force the insurer to pay for a repair that exceeds the car’s actual cash value if the policy lets them total it. Total-loss thresholds vary by state and are sometimes a formula rather than a fixed percent. When a car is totaled, the title that comes back is a brand. What that brand does to the next sale is the subject of the <a href="/guides/title/salvage-title-guide/">salvage-title guide</a>. If you keep a totaled car, ask what the salvage bid was and what the rebuilt-title inspection in your state requires. Keeping it is sometimes sensible and sometimes a project you did not intend to start.
</p>
<p>
  An open recall does not, by itself, void a policy. It can matter in a claim if the unrepaired defect caused the loss, and it is a free repair regardless. Check the VIN at the <a href="/recall-checker/">recall checker</a> when you bind the policy. NHTSA’s description of the manufacturer’s duty is at <a href="https://www.nhtsa.gov/recalls" rel="noopener">nhtsa.gov/recalls</a>.
</p>
<p>
  Gap coverage, if you owe more than the car’s value, is the difference between the loan payoff and the insurer’s actual-cash-value check. Your auto insurer and the dealer’s finance office may sell different versions. Price both against the same payoff letter. The <a href="/guides/financing/">financing hub</a> covers when that gap exists in the first place, which is mostly long terms and rolled-in negative equity.
</p>

<h2>Shopping without fooling yourself</h2>
<p>
  Quote three carriers on the same limits, the same deductibles, and the same VIN, on the same day if you can. A quote from last quarter is a different market. When you switch, do not cancel the old policy until the new one shows an effective date and time, and tell the lender if a lienholder has to be listed. A lapse is expensive later, because prior-insurance history is one of the inputs.
</p>
<p>
  If a premium jumps at renewal, ask which factor moved: a claim, a ticket, a ZIP-code rate change, or a mileage correction. You can shop that renewal the same way you shopped the first policy. Loyalty is not a coverage. The state insurance department will also take a complaint if a cancellation or a non-renewal does not match state rules. That department, not a comparison site, is the regulator.
</p>
<p>
  We may link to a quote service. That link is paid, it is marked, and it does not change the advice above. The declarations page is still the document. If a page on this site ever implies a specific percent you will save, treat that as a mistake and write to us. The honest instruction is to compare the contracts.
</p>

